  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1206 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nToday through Monday\n\nSouthwest winds will continue to bring near-record to record \nwarmth northward into the region through Monday. 850 mb\ntemperatures will range from 18C to 21C. These temperatures are\n2 to 2.5 standard deviations above-normal. Like much of last\nweek, the NBM has been on the cool side on its high temperatures.\nAs a result, continued with doing a 50 50 blend of the NBM and \nNBM 90th percentile to bring up these temperatures. On Sunday, \nthis blend still looked to be too cool so added some 850 mb \nmixed- down (which ranged from the mid-80s to near 90) to bump \nthe high temperatures a couple of degrees higher. High\ntemperatures will be in the lower and mid-80s on both days, and\nlow temperatures will be in the 60s and lower 70s.\n\nMonday Night\n\nA cold front will move southeast through the area. With this\nfront moving through the area a bit slower, there could be some\nwarm low records along and south of Interstate 90 for October\n12. \n\nThe better low-level moisture continues to be north of\nInterstate 94. As a result, went with a 20-40 percent of light\nrain showers between Interstates 90 and 94, and anywhere from a\n40 to 70 percent north of Interstate 94. Rainfall amounts still\nlook to be on the light side. There is a 40-70 percent\nprobability of a tenth of an inch or more north of Interstate 94\nand around a 20 percent chance of those rainfall amounts near\nInterstate 90.\n\nRemainder of the week\n\nMore seasonable temperatures will return on Tuesday and linger\nthrough Thursday with high temperatures mainly in the 60s and\nlow temperatures ranging from the upper 30s to lower 50s. There\nare few ensemble members even colder at the end of the week, but\nconfidence is very low (less than a 20 percent) that they will \noccur.\n\nThe NBM has some low rain chances from the remnants of Hurricane\nSimon getting as far north as northeast Iowa and far southern\nWisconsin. Many of the operational global models keeps this\nmoisture much further south, so kept these rain chances less\nthan a 20 percent.",
